d.div:
You with qualities of a treasure protector
and with the nature of Shingja Chen;
wrathful Apsé, like a butcher slaughtering a sheep,
your function to kill those with broken vows;
Red Jackal Face, the color of your enemy’s blood:
these and other protectors, fulfill all wishes without hindrance.[51]

f.d:
The Vedas, together with the six branches and the two subsidiaries, all emerged from the faces of Brahmā, because of which the text continues:

Therefore, from the central face of Brahmā came Āyurveda;
the Sāmaveda, Ṛgveda, and Yajurveda,
were spoken from the other faces.[87]
Also:

From his mouth came the brahman priestly caste,
from the shoulders the kṣatriya lordly caste,
from the thighs the vaiśya merchant class,
from the feet came the śūdra lower class.[88]
Therefore, the religion of the brahman caste, whose place of birth has the highest status, is the Vedas.

d.div:
From the waters first appeared Śrī and others;
the eight śubha jewels, which when prayed to in the morning,
banish the fearful prophecies of bad dreams and so forth;

then the horses that pulled the sun’s chariot;
surā, drink of the gods; the kaustubha jewel; beautiful earrings;
medicine known as ambrosia, and ambrosia itself;

div.div:
In spring take food that is hot, coarse, and warm;
in summer take that which is oily, warm, salty, and sour;
in autumn take that which is cold, sweet, and oily;
and in winter take that which is coarse, sour, oily, and sweet.
If food, drink, and medicine taken at these four times
accords with the above, few illnesses will arise.

For a wind illness give oily food,
for bile a purgative is good,
for phlegm give an emetic.
Wind, bile, and phlegm arising together
is known as a combination;
for such give all three treatments.

Among those with a combination,
some will have two, some will have all three;
whichever predominates will be their nature.

d.div:
Pointed myrobalan has three sides,
Fearless has five;
Life-giving is also known as Golden,
Ambrosia as Fleshy;
Purity is known as Fleshless,
Increase as Pale Yellow;
Victorious is like the kuva fruit.

Āmla is fleshy, with little inner structure,
of strong taste, and found on rocky hills;
well-ripened fruit is the best.

f.d:
Āryadeva said;

Durdharṣa, come out of samādhi,
the sun has set over the mountain,
the people too are getting tired;
apart from talk, what else is left?

Thus, unmatched in wisdom, compassion, and power, his true nature is the illusory dance of the gnosis of Buddha Vairocana. He is inseparable from Vajrapāṇi, the keeper of secrets. He is Ānanda, attendant of our Teacher and compiler of all his teachings, come again for the glory of living beings. This great manifested being, Vairocana, requested many medical texts from the Kashmiri Candranandana. In particular, as the fourth chapter of the Crystal Rock Chronicle says:

Vairocana translated Bön and Dharma;
Vairocana translated medicine and astronomy;
Vairocana translated sutra and tantra.
In the sutra translations he is written as Yeshé Dé;
in the tantra translations he is written as Vairocana;
in the Bön translations he is written as Genjak Thangta;
in the astronomy translations he is Indra Vairocana;
in the medical translations he is Chöbar Jawa—
he translated most of sutra and its commentaries.
As this quote indicates he translated various medical texts including Instructions on Medicinal Plants, also known as the Hundred Thousand Medicinal Plants of Mañjuśrī, and Fungi Therapy for Poisons by the ācārya Sūryasiṃha.

The Lotus Chronicle says:

Even those not traveled to India are claimed as translators;
the wise and holy of Tibet wander to the ends of the earth;
a new law of patronage is made with the Mongolians;
black inside, white outside, like whirls of smoke,[382]
the merits of Tibet decline and decline—
these signs will appear when it is time to unearth
this hidden treasure of Lotus Crystal Cave,
and the treasure finder will be Ramo Shelmen.[383]

The emanated treasure finder Dorbum Chökyi Drakpa is listed in Stream of the Ganges: An Account of Teachings Received by my lama, Ngawang Losang Gyatso, in reality the omniscient one. Dorbum’s Great Vase of Ambrosia yellow scrolls, so called because it can be compared to one’s own heart or to a wish-fulfilling jewel, he most likely received in northern Tradüntsé from the heart of Hayagrīva who Eclipses the Arrogant. He also recovered some supplementary material hidden separately under the stone statue in Jamtrin temple. This work is comprised of four collections:
1. The root, or mother collection; outwardly a catalog of contents;
2. The inner, son documents, which are presented as an intermediate step[384] and which include the arrowhead teachings among other teachings;
3. The no-step collection, dealing with topics not clearly listed in the contents chapter and the stepped collection;
4. The mind-treasure collection on the essentials for the practice of the other three.

d.div:
This illusory body, this aggregate of form,
is in the nature of the meditation deities;
its material residue purified in its own abode.
Like the rainbow it cannot be found;
like the dance of the moon reflected in water,
it appears where it will.

The first of these, the tradition of Tongmen, includes
Commentary to Differentiating the Middle Way and the Extremes and other works by translator Jñānamitra;
Collection on Knowledge by Dharmapāla;
Magic Mirror Compilation on Pulse and Urine by Künga Wangchuk;

Treatment of Growths by Tsojé Drupa;
Structure of the Torso by Shāka Lodrö;
Yeshé Wangchuk’s Ambrosia Drops Collection on Gaklhok Treatments, and so on;
Treatments for Women’s Diseases: Fulfilling to Behold composed by Palgön Sherap;

Treatment for Urinary Stones and so forth by Jangchup Pal;
Magic Circle of Water Surgical Removal by Samten Sherap;
Elixirs by Öser Gönpo;
Commentaries to the Root, Explanatory, and Final Tantras, with annotations to the Instruction Tantra, and so forth, by Tongla Takchung;
Types of Elixir by Künga Nyingpo;
Essence of Eight Branches by Palshé;

Treatment of Infectious Diseases by Dondrup Gönpo;
Treatments for Poisoning by Lekden Gönpo;
Compilation of Miscellaneous Treatments by Jamyang;
Secret Commentary to the Heart Tantra by Sherap Öser;

Texts composed by Nya and his followers include:
True Illumination Commentary on the Heart Tantra
and Six Sections Explaining Reversal Treatment;
Refutation of Arguments by Jangchup Sherap;
and Essence of Differentiating the Middle Way and the Extremes and so on, by Tsöndrü Sengé.

Drangti Dréu composed History and others;
Trikhu composed Six Chapters on Fever;
and Drangti Gyalnyé wrote Secret Treasury of Ambrosia among others.

Yuthok Gönpo Sherap composed Ambrosia Essence Instructions and so on;
Gönpo Rinchen composed Son Teachings in Ninety-Four Chapters;
Yuthok Gönpo Dorjé had Auxiliary Texts for the Tantra, among others;
Khyungwo composed Commentary on Eight Branches;
and Gönpo Drakpa compiled Collection on Practice.

Biji Leklo composed Essentials of Knowledge and others;
Palchen composed Three Fundamentals Collections;
Lhuné composed Ocean Garland Treatments for the Head;
Marlo composed the Peacock and Parrot collections;
and Sherap Lodrö composed Treatments for Naga Illness and Essential Instructions.

Chejé Gyalwa wrote Charms and Essentials of Knowledge;[578]
Lhasé composed Ambrosia Essence;
Gönseng composed Brief Work on the Abdomen;

Takla Gah composed Laying Out the Keys to Eight Branches, among others;
Tisang composed Essentials of Eight Branches;
Ulang composed History of Medicine;
and Shikpo composed Fourfold Exposition Collection.

Ukpa Chösang taught Crystal Jewel Commentary and so on;
Wangchuk taught Commentary on the Causes of Purification;
Rinchen Gyalpo taught Collections;
and Chösé composed Jewel Heap of Miscellany.
These were taught by the traditions
known as those of the nine wise Tibetans.

1. Śāntigarbha propagated medicine and treatment for leprosy;
2. Hvashang Mahāyāna propagated cauterization and stick treatment;
3. Tsenpashila propagated cupping treatment, dietary rules, behavior, and other practices;
4. Drugu Sengdo’s treatments for horses;

5. Khyölma Rutsé’s treatment of broken limbs;
6. Dharmaśīla’s special treatment for poisoning;
7. Lotsāwa Rinchen Sangpo’s teaching tradition of the eight branches;
8. Puruṣa Agnideva’s extensive teachings on practice;

9. Purang Nyangmen’s extensive use of the surgical spoon;
10. Tongmen Takchung’s special treatments for fevers;
11. Ukpa Chösang’s splints for limbs;
12. Biji Yorwo’s reversal treatment;

13. Chejé Nagu’s special treatments for women;
14. Drangti Sekhyung’s clear anatomy of the torso;
15. Minyak Dawa Drakpa’s Great Compilation;
16. Thazhi Śākya Dar’s explicit treatments on stone disorders;

17. Nyenak Köntön’s treatments for ligaments and joints;
18. Sumen Tashi’s treatment for chest illnesses;
19. Chejé Draksö’s removal of arrowheads;
20. Thekar Gyalwa Rinchen’s surgical treatment for opening the eyes;

21. Gyimjang Gokar’s cauterization of growths;
22. Jomo Namo’s compress treatment of arrow wounds;
23. Dartön Gendün’s commentary on the essentials of hot and cold disorders;
24. Drupchen Orgyen’s preparation of mercury;

25. Rongtön Chösang’s compilation of practices;
26. Rongtön Shewang’s extensive medical plant preparation;
27. Geshé Rokchung’s extensive and concise works on remedies for poisoning;
28. Bamen Tsönwang’s treatments for limb bones;

29. Limen Sizin’s surgery for the head;
30. Gongjé Mentsün’s examination of signs;
31. Tratsang Druklha’s teachings on charms and illness-causing spirits;
32. Drapa Ngönshé’s explicit teachings on symptoms;

33. Shangtön Siji’s main points;
34. Geshé Suktön’s practice by reference to texts;
35. Purang Zemen’s compress treatments for the head;
36. Sumen Josö’s Chinese magical cures;

37. Chemen Jodor’s treatment for limb wounds;
38. Bamen Jothok’s complete treatment for fevers;
39. Sokgong Merin’s practices of the eight branches;
40. Dromen Tashi’s head and eye surgery;

41. Chuktsam Darsö’s magical treatments for the abdomen;
42. Dokmen Samten’s special treatment for gaklhok;
43. Bhenak Mebar’s the use of the substitution pulse;[579]
44. Shangdrung Lodrö’s tinder cauterization instructions;

45. Kukpa Gotum’s arrowhead instructions;
46. Khyungpo Gyina’s extensive teachings on remedies for poisons;
47. Rakshak Tertön’s miscellaneous treatments;
48. Bamen Chenmo’s removal and drying up of pus;

49. Jemen Shalu’s structure of the abdomen;
50. Tongmen Sengdrak’s teaching tradition on urinary stones;
51. Chikmen Gawo’s Words of a Simpleton teachings;
52. Nupmen Josö’s general treatments for poisoning;

Feted by the great, I have abandoned all pride; mistreated for the sake of others, I am not disheartened;
having given up all thoughts of praise and respect,
I am known as Rishi Mipaksam (Wish-Granting Tree for Humanity).

Difficult to obtain medicines I have produced with hardship and effort;
those easy to produce I have cast off laziness and gathered them;
fulfilling the hopes and wishes of all those to be cured,
I am known as Rishi Menjin (Giver of Medicine).

Unattached to power and impartial;
holding as equal those who like me and those who loathe me,
the high and the low, the rich and the poor;
not working in search of gratitude,
I am known as Genyen Tsojé (Upāsaka[599] Doctor).

Toward the wise physicians I give praise and respect;
toward the inexperienced I have no disdain and instead rejoice;
toward my peers I surrender all thoughts of jealousy;
I am known as Rishi Sempa Leksem (Brave One of Good Mind).

Reducing to shreds the net cast by the lord of death;
forcing out the fearsome and poisonous arrows of pain;
transforming old age, brought on by bodily deterioration, into youth;
I am known as Rishi Mijik Sokjin (Giver of Life and Fearlessness).

Illnesses easy to cure are treated with medicine, diet, and behavior;
for more difficult ones, reversal treatments and swift magical remedies are recommended;
for neither of the above, charm circles and mantras are taught;
I am known, therefore, as Rishi Thaptsül Pakmé (Of Limitless Methods).

I compose works illuminating the thoughts of Medicine Buddha;
I give teachings to open the wisdom eyes of my followers;
I wield the sword that cleaves the arguments of wrong ideas;
I am known as Rishi Sorik Tenzin (Preserver of the Doctrine of Medicine).

I nourish the healthy with protections and suitable elixirs;
I nourish the sick, their humors disturbed, back to health;
I nourish future lives with virtues to be cultivated and wrongs to be discarded;
I am known, therefore, as Rishi Taktu Tsojé (Constantly Nourishing).

A refuge for the wretched, wherever and whoever they be;
an affectionate mind, like a mother or father, toward all strangers;
all activities of body, speech, and mind given solely to helping others,
I am known as Rishi Drowai Phama (Mother and Father of Sentient Beings).

Toward pus, blood, excrement, and urine I am like a dog or a pig;
doing everything for the sick, I am like their servant;
to their relatives I am the wish-fulfilling cow;
I am known as Rishi Drowai Khölpo (Servant of Living Beings).

Having found conviction in the pure view of the world and its beings;
understanding that initiations are to be received, pledges to be guarded;
seeing the two stages as the life channel of Vajrayana;
I am known as Rishi Chimé Nyingpo (Essence of Deathlessness).

Many lives I have trained in the tradition of medicine;
in India and Tibet I have helped sentient beings.
In this way I have pledged to guide living beings;
I am known, therefore, as a rishi of many names.

f.d:
As it says in the Twenty Verses on Vows of the Bodhisattva:

For intermediate downfalls confess to three,
for the remainder confess before one,
for other afflictions and non-afflictions,
confess just before your own mind.[845]
Therefore in the presence of a person suitable for confession, you confess from the depths of your heart and rise up from the fault.

Aggregates are entities of the five buddhas;
to despise them is the eighth.[912]
The five aggregates of form, feelings, discrimination, formations, and consciousness have the entities or nature of the five naturally pure buddhas or tathāgatas—Vairocana, Ratnasambhava, Amitābha, Amoghasiddhi, and Akṣobhya, respectively. They are to be given offerings with the inner fire-offering ritual and should be treated well and made to feel at ease. The eighth root downfall is to deliberately despise or torment the body, and to foolishly pursue behavior such as self-denial, casting yourself into pits, demeaning acts, improper feats of endurance, self-mutilation, and fasting—with a mind not grasped by the Mahayana and seeing these practices as causes for liberation.[913]

“Well skilled” means manually, verbally, and mentally skilled.
Skilled with his hands, he is wise in medical techniques;
with sweet words he is capable of bringing joy to the sick;
of bright intellect, his mind is clear and unconfused.
Knowing these, he becomes a master among craftsman.[1034]
“Well skilled” from the six causes means he is manually skillful, verbally skillful, and mentally skillful. Skilled with his hands, he knows techniques of medical preparation and medical practice, such as using the enema medicine pipe, bloodletting lancets, and splints. Being verbally skilled in using pleasant and reassuring words, he is able to bring joy to patients. Being of a bright intellect, he remains clear and unconfused in the recognition of symptoms, medicines, medical techniques, and so on. Knowing these he becomes a master or supreme among craftsmen.

As for the etymology of physician:
“medicine” by curing illness, aiding the body;
“hero” by applying surgery to the disease;
“physician” by protecting all beings like a father;
regarded as lords by kings.[1070]
This is the etymology of the term physician (sman pa/dpa’).[1071] Because he alleviates the pain of illness and brings relief to the body, he is “medicine” (sman). When he uses surgical instruments such as surgical spoons and cutters for serious illnesses, he is brave and fearless (pa/dpa’). Therefore he is a “hero of medicine” (sman pa/dpa’). Alternatively, he is a “physician” because he protects all living beings like a father.[1072] Also, kings are as gods of the world, and even they revere physicians as lords. Therefore, they are known as lhajé, or “lords of the gods.”

In the Ten Cakra Kṣitigarbha Sutra it says:

With undistracted faith and respect listen to the Dharma;
do not belittle or disparage it.
Make offerings to the Dharma teacher;
see him as being like a buddha.[1121]

In the Sutra of Great Play:

It is possible to encounter no Dharma
for a hundred thousand million eons,
and yet you have found it today;
dispel all inattentiveness.[1129]
Therefore, it is very important to embrace the conduct of listening to Dharma conscientiously with body, speech, and mind.

From the Garland of Past Lives:

Dispelling the darkness of ignorance, listening is a lantern;
never stolen by thieves and robbers, it is the greatest wealth;
destroying the foes of confusion, it is a great weapon;
showing ways and giving advice, it is the greatest friend,
the greatest glory, the greatest fame, and the greatest treasure;
when meeting those of high status, it is the greatest gift;
in the midst of others, it pleases the learned;
shining on opponents, it is like the light of the sun.[1134]
Also:

You become disciplined and grow clarity of confidence;
fame and reputation quickly arrive.
It is the cause of a very special ease and skill in speech.
With the confidence and fearlessness of listening,
you clearly understand the meanings.
Endowed with listening you dwell
without contradiction on the path of the three circles.
It bequeaths essence to practice,
and you attain liberation without hardship
from the fortress of birth.[1135]
